1
00:00:25,759 --> 00:00:28,126
♪♪

2
00:00:36,436 --> 00:00:38,302
♪♪

3
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,409
♪♪

4
00:01:04,498 --> 00:01:06,543
♪♪

5
00:01:44,371 --> 00:01:46,203
♪♪

6
00:02:04,291 --> 00:02:06,269
The lab here at
the medical center

7
00:02:06,293 --> 00:02:08,438
ran 11 different tests.

8
00:02:08,462 --> 00:02:11,441
In all the tests, the
results were the same:

9
00:02:11,465 --> 00:02:16,245
No trace of steroids was
found in Billy Leon's system.

10
00:02:16,269 --> 00:02:17,447
It can be stated conclusively

11
00:02:17,471 --> 00:02:20,350
that he is not using
steroids at the present time,

12
00:02:20,374 --> 00:02:23,453
nor has he used them
in the past six months.

13
00:02:23,477 --> 00:02:26,356
Despite rumors to the contrary,

14
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,192
Mr. Leon's excellent physical
condition appears to be

15
00:02:29,216 --> 00:02:30,860
nothing more than the result

16
00:02:30,884 --> 00:02:34,263
of good old-fashioned
hard work and determination.

17
00:02:34,287 --> 00:02:35,965
Thank you.

18
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:38,568
Now, you heard the
man... Billy here is clean.

19
00:02:38,592 --> 00:02:39,769
He earned the title

20
00:02:39,793 --> 00:02:42,605
of heavyweight champion,
and make no mistake,

21
00:02:42,629 --> 00:02:45,608
he's gonna keep
that title next month

22
00:02:45,632 --> 00:02:49,278
when he fights Boomer Wilson at
the fabulous Crown Resort Hotel

23
00:02:49,302 --> 00:02:50,947
- in Atlanta!
- Get him, champ!

24
00:02:50,971 --> 00:02:52,582
Now that all this steroid stuff

25
00:02:52,606 --> 00:02:54,517
has been laid to
rest once and for all,

26
00:02:54,541 --> 00:02:56,686
maybe you guys could
go back to writing the truth,

27
00:02:56,710 --> 00:02:59,255
which is that Billy
Leon is the world's best

28
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:00,790
damn heavyweight boxer!

29
00:03:00,814 --> 00:03:03,092
We're gonna see in
Atlanta! Thank you!

30
00:03:03,116 --> 00:03:04,394
That's enough, folks.

31
00:03:04,418 --> 00:03:06,444
That's enough!

32
00:03:08,255 --> 00:03:10,400
No, no time. I'm sorry, but
thank you for your concern.

33
00:03:10,424 --> 00:03:12,335
Okay, folks, party
time. Let's get home.

34
00:03:12,359 --> 00:03:14,237
Let's get packed.
Let's get to Atlanta.

35
00:03:14,261 --> 00:03:15,805
- He was here.
- Who was here?

36
00:03:15,829 --> 00:03:18,541
Nick Underwood, that little worm
that started this mess was here!

37
00:03:18,565 --> 00:03:20,810
Of course he was here. He
broke the story. It'd be weird

38
00:03:20,834 --> 00:03:22,178
for him not to be here.

39
00:03:22,202 --> 00:03:24,113
Everybody would think that
he made the whole thing up.

40
00:03:24,137 --> 00:03:26,049
- He did make it up!
- Come on, Billy, it's over.

41
00:03:26,073 --> 00:03:27,984
- Let it rest.
- I can't! I thought the guy was a friend

42
00:03:28,008 --> 00:03:29,452
- of mine, and he suckered me!
- Alana, can

43
00:03:29,476 --> 00:03:30,787
- you tell us how you feel...?
- Billy, forget it!

44
00:03:30,811 --> 00:03:32,588
This story's not your
problem anymore, it's his!

45
00:03:32,612 --> 00:03:34,323
When this story comes out
and they find out what happened,

46
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:36,325
Nick Underwood's gonna
have a lot of explaining to do.

47
00:03:36,349 --> 00:03:37,927
Please, get in the
car. Just get in the car.

48
00:03:37,951 --> 00:03:39,362
- You got a moment, Billy?
- The man is scum.

49
00:03:39,386 --> 00:03:41,097
It's like something from
the bottom of your shoe.

50
00:03:41,121 --> 00:03:43,466
What happened was I
got fed false information,

51
00:03:43,490 --> 00:03:45,001
that's what happened.

52
00:03:45,025 --> 00:03:47,603
Of course... I confirmed
it with <i>two</i> doctors.

53
00:03:47,627 --> 00:03:49,806
They must have
been paid to lie to me.

54
00:03:49,830 --> 00:03:51,641
I mean, that's all I can figure.

55
00:03:51,665 --> 00:03:54,377
No, but I'm sure gonna find out.

56
00:03:54,401 --> 00:03:57,847
Oh, no, no, come on, Bill,
don't, don't print an apology.

57
00:03:57,871 --> 00:04:00,650
That's gonna make
me look so stupid.

58
00:04:00,674 --> 00:04:02,518
Look, give me three weeks, okay?

59
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:04,887
I'll find out who was
responsible for this.

60
00:04:04,911 --> 00:04:08,124
I'll make him write the
retraction, I swear it.

61
00:04:08,148 --> 00:04:09,759
Okay.

62
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:11,327
Okay, you're a peach.

63
00:04:11,351 --> 00:04:13,013
Ciao for now.

64
00:04:19,259 --> 00:04:22,538
It's 3:45, Wednesday the 25th.

65
00:04:22,562 --> 00:04:26,309
I've got three weeks to
figure out who set me up.

66
00:04:26,333 --> 00:04:28,678
The people I talked to
were totally convincing,

67
00:04:28,702 --> 00:04:31,314
therefore, they must
have been coached by...

68
00:04:31,338 --> 00:04:33,916
someone who knows Billy well.

69
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:37,286
Therefore, I am going to
find out everything I can

70
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:40,872
about Mr. Leon's
close personal friends.

71
00:04:59,132 --> 00:05:01,144
Hey, Billy, you're looking good!

72
00:05:03,203 --> 00:05:05,604
Good jab! Hit with the jab!

73
00:05:07,541 --> 00:05:09,519
Nice left.

74
00:05:15,382 --> 00:05:17,374
One minute to go.

75
00:05:18,985 --> 00:05:20,463
All right, let's go! Come on!

76
00:05:20,487 --> 00:05:23,099
Come on, champ! Who's
the champ? Who's the champ?

77
00:05:23,123 --> 00:05:24,167
Underneath!

78
00:05:24,191 --> 00:05:25,234
You're headhunting,
Billy. Go to...

79
00:05:25,258 --> 00:05:26,569
I want some Billy stuff here.

80
00:05:26,593 --> 00:05:29,505
Who's the champ?
Who's the champ?

81
00:05:29,529 --> 00:05:32,041
I just got off the phone
with the cable company.

82
00:05:32,065 --> 00:05:33,209
Oh, that's sweet, champ!

83
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:35,444
- That's sweet!
- 30% increase in subscriptions.

84
00:05:35,468 --> 00:05:38,302
Wow, you must
be kicking yourself.

85
00:05:39,472 --> 00:05:40,950
Yeah? Why is that?

86
00:05:40,974 --> 00:05:44,587
For not negotiating
a better cut.

87
00:05:44,611 --> 00:05:47,590
Next time, Alana. Next time.

88
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:52,228
All right, that's it. Take five.

89
00:05:52,252 --> 00:05:54,664
Take five, Billy. Way to go.

90
00:05:54,688 --> 00:05:57,033
- You okay, Nate?
- Yeah.

91
00:05:57,057 --> 00:05:58,234
Nice.

92
00:05:58,258 --> 00:06:00,336
- How's the eye feel, Billy?
- It feels okay.

93
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:02,338
Now, you gotta
protect that, kid.

94
00:06:02,362 --> 00:06:04,273
- It'll open up fast.
- Yeah.

95
00:06:04,297 --> 00:06:07,009
- Way to go, champ.
- Good workout. Good workout.

96
00:06:07,033 --> 00:06:08,511
Get him out of here!

97
00:06:08,535 --> 00:06:10,313
Looking good, Billy. Can
I talk to you for a minute?

98
00:06:10,337 --> 00:06:11,414
- You want to talk?
- Yeah.

99
00:06:11,438 --> 00:06:12,982
Preferably in private.

100
00:06:13,006 --> 00:06:15,318
- I'm gonna break your head!
- Come on.

101
00:06:15,342 --> 00:06:17,253
Get him out of here, will
ya? Somebody get him out!

102
00:06:17,277 --> 00:06:19,422
- Listen...
- I found out some things, Billy.

103
00:06:19,446 --> 00:06:21,324
- Get out! -Nick...
- Will you listen to me?

104
00:06:21,348 --> 00:06:22,692
- I can explain what happened!
- Take a walk, Nick.

105
00:06:22,716 --> 00:06:23,693
I don't want to hear you!

106
00:06:23,717 --> 00:06:25,094
- I don't want to see you!
- He said good-bye, Nick.

107
00:06:25,118 --> 00:06:26,718
- Get out of my face!
- He said good-bye!

108
00:06:36,863 --> 00:06:38,889
Nick's crazy.

109
00:07:02,656 --> 00:07:03,866
Good. Well, gonna
see you at the fight.

110
00:07:03,890 --> 00:07:06,035
Thank you very much for coming.

111
00:07:06,059 --> 00:07:09,171
Alana, you look
positively edible.

112
00:07:09,195 --> 00:07:10,640
Ooh. Thank you, Clayton.

113
00:07:10,664 --> 00:07:11,641
How do I look?

114
00:07:11,665 --> 00:07:14,577
The cumberbund is
fastened in the back,

115
00:07:14,601 --> 00:07:16,178
- not in the front.
- The what?

116
00:07:16,202 --> 00:07:18,514
- Help him.
- Oh, Mickey, it's not a back support.

117
00:07:18,538 --> 00:07:20,650
Where's my guest of honor?

118
00:07:20,674 --> 00:07:23,252
That's just what
I was wondering.

119
00:07:23,276 --> 00:07:24,820
I don't remember inviting you.

120
00:07:24,844 --> 00:07:26,322
An oversight.

121
00:07:26,346 --> 00:07:27,857
Would you do me a favor, please?

122
00:07:27,881 --> 00:07:30,660
Would you get, like, a security
guard over here, please?

123
00:07:30,684 --> 00:07:32,662
Thank you very much.

124
00:07:32,686 --> 00:07:35,431
You like being thrown
out of every place you go?

125
00:07:35,455 --> 00:07:38,634
No. As a matter of fact,
I don't like it at all, really.

126
00:07:38,658 --> 00:07:40,803
But that's how badly
I want to talk to Billy.

127
00:07:40,827 --> 00:07:43,873
Who is, uh, where, by the way?

128
00:07:43,897 --> 00:07:46,042
He'll be back later...
He went for a run.

129
00:07:46,066 --> 00:07:47,076
Yeah, my orders.

130
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:48,411
I thought it would
settle him down

131
00:07:48,435 --> 00:07:49,612
after what happened today.

132
00:07:49,636 --> 00:07:51,314
Get his mind back to boxing,

133
00:07:51,338 --> 00:07:54,083
not beating the tar out of
headline-grabbing sportswriters.

134
00:07:54,107 --> 00:07:55,818
Don't count me out yet.

135
00:07:55,842 --> 00:07:57,386
As I've been
trying to tell Billy,

136
00:07:57,410 --> 00:07:58,788
I made some very
interesting discoveries

137
00:07:58,812 --> 00:08:01,524
about those nearest
and dearest to him.

138
00:08:01,548 --> 00:08:04,627
My next story is gonna
knock your socks off.

139
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:08,164
Would you remove Mr. Underwood
from the premises, please?

140
00:08:08,188 --> 00:08:10,433
- I... I believe he's trespassing.
- Ah.

141
00:08:10,457 --> 00:08:13,202
Registered guest.

142
00:08:13,226 --> 00:08:14,904
Ciao for now.

143
00:08:14,928 --> 00:08:16,238
Ciao.

144
00:08:16,262 --> 00:08:19,255
Very nice color on
you, Mickey, very nice.

145
00:08:21,735 --> 00:08:23,328
Always a pleasure.

146
00:08:42,889 --> 00:08:46,669
It's 8:15 p.m., Friday the 11th.

147
00:08:46,693 --> 00:08:48,270
I went over to
Clayton's cocktail party

148
00:08:48,294 --> 00:08:50,806
to try and talk to Billy,
but he wasn't there,

149
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:53,042
and I wound up
getting the bum's rush.

150
00:08:53,066 --> 00:08:54,710
But I'm gonna keep on trying.

151
00:08:54,734 --> 00:08:56,178
I owe him that much.

152
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:05,008
Hello?

153
00:09:06,246 --> 00:09:08,511
I thought you might call.

154
00:09:09,616 --> 00:09:12,495
Sure, if you want to.

155
00:09:12,519 --> 00:09:14,263
9:00?

156
00:09:14,287 --> 00:09:16,119
9:00 it is.

157
00:09:21,795 --> 00:09:22,838
Bingo.

158
00:09:34,741 --> 00:09:36,710
Excuse me.

159
00:09:42,916 --> 00:09:44,748
Housekeeping.

160
00:09:51,491 --> 00:09:53,335
Excuse me, sir,

161
00:09:53,359 --> 00:09:55,971
would you like your
bed turned down?

162
00:09:55,995 --> 00:09:57,657
Sir?

163
00:10:26,292 --> 00:10:28,771
Oh. Mr. Matlock.

164
00:10:28,795 --> 00:10:31,507
Monroe Weber of Stewart,
Tidyman, Herzog & Harris.

165
00:10:31,531 --> 00:10:33,509
Look, thank you for coming down.

166
00:10:33,533 --> 00:10:36,645
Uh, our main area of
expertise lies in contract law,

167
00:10:36,669 --> 00:10:39,582
not criminal law, I'm afraid.

168
00:10:39,606 --> 00:10:41,484
I'm not making any promises.

169
00:10:41,508 --> 00:10:42,485
Mm-hmm.

170
00:10:42,509 --> 00:10:44,153
You said there'd be some coffee.

171
00:10:44,177 --> 00:10:47,957
Whole pot of coffee...
It's right in there with Billy.

172
00:10:47,981 --> 00:10:50,559
Why can't these things
happen in the daytime?

173
00:10:50,583 --> 00:10:54,145
Well, 72% of all homicides
happen after 8:00 p.m.

174
00:10:56,389 --> 00:10:57,982
- Huh.
- Mm-hmm.

175
00:10:59,726 --> 00:11:02,138
I hope Billy hasn't
drunk all the coffee.

176
00:11:02,162 --> 00:11:03,572
Me, too.

177
00:11:03,596 --> 00:11:06,242
Uh, anyway, Mr. Underwood
caught a bullet through the chest.

178
00:11:06,266 --> 00:11:08,911
The police are pretty
sure it came from a gun

179
00:11:08,935 --> 00:11:11,547
they found in a Dumpster
on the hotel grounds...

180
00:11:11,571 --> 00:11:13,983
Billy's gun.

181
00:11:14,007 --> 00:11:15,718
He travels with a gun?

182
00:11:15,742 --> 00:11:17,052
Protection.

183
00:11:17,076 --> 00:11:19,555
A lot of nuts out there,
especially if you're famous.

184
00:11:19,579 --> 00:11:21,991
Look, here's the
police report. Go on in.

185
00:11:22,015 --> 00:11:25,679
If there's anything I can
help you with, I'll be right here.

186
00:11:36,696 --> 00:11:39,375
I went for a nice,
long, slow run,

187
00:11:39,399 --> 00:11:41,410
just like Mickey told me to.

188
00:11:41,434 --> 00:11:43,812
I got done a little after 9:00.

189
00:11:43,836 --> 00:11:46,048
I went to my bungalow,
took a shower

190
00:11:46,072 --> 00:11:48,951
so I could get over to the
party Clayton was throwing.

191
00:11:48,975 --> 00:11:51,787
Next thing I know,
there's a bunch of sirens

192
00:11:51,811 --> 00:11:54,957
and... people
running all around.

193
00:11:54,981 --> 00:11:57,993
Pretty soon my wife
comes in and tells me

194
00:11:58,017 --> 00:12:00,629
that somebody just
shot Nick Underwood.

195
00:12:00,653 --> 00:12:03,966
Next thing I know, the police
are knocking at my door,

196
00:12:03,990 --> 00:12:06,702
asking whether this gun
they were holding was mine.

197
00:12:06,726 --> 00:12:09,205
I said, "No, mine's right here."

198
00:12:09,229 --> 00:12:11,507
Only, when I opened
the drawer it was in,

199
00:12:11,531 --> 00:12:14,109
it was gone.

200
00:12:14,133 --> 00:12:16,762
About then they started
reading me my rights.

201
00:12:20,173 --> 00:12:23,085
How many people knew you
kept your gun in your room?

202
00:12:23,109 --> 00:12:25,554
It wasn't any big secret,
if that's what you mean.

203
00:12:25,578 --> 00:12:27,156
Figured if people knew I had it,

204
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,358
they wouldn't mess with me.

205
00:12:30,383 --> 00:12:33,329
You leave your door locked
or unlocked when you were

206
00:12:33,353 --> 00:12:35,464
- on that run?
- Unlocked.

207
00:12:35,488 --> 00:12:37,866
Lose stuff a lot when
I run... Keys and stuff.

208
00:12:37,890 --> 00:12:38,867
Hmm.

209
00:12:38,891 --> 00:12:42,338
Well, the maid said
she saw you running

210
00:12:42,362 --> 00:12:44,974
from the direction of Nick
Underwood's bungalow

211
00:12:44,998 --> 00:12:47,710
just before she found the body.

212
00:12:47,734 --> 00:12:50,346
She saw me running?

213
00:12:50,370 --> 00:12:52,448
That's all! I didn't
even know the guy

214
00:12:52,472 --> 00:12:53,949
had a bungalow, damn it!

215
00:12:53,973 --> 00:12:57,353
I'd just got done telling him I
was gonna tear his head off.

216
00:12:57,377 --> 00:13:00,745
Any sane person would've
been on the first plane out of there!

217
00:13:02,015 --> 00:13:03,859
Take it easy, son!

218
00:13:03,883 --> 00:13:05,327
I didn't kill him,
I'm telling you!

219
00:13:05,351 --> 00:13:09,331
I didn't kill him!
I didn't kill him!

220
00:13:13,293 --> 00:13:15,404
- I didn't kill him!
- Calm down!

221
00:13:15,428 --> 00:13:16,639
I didn't kill him!

222
00:13:16,663 --> 00:13:18,107
That man's a stick of dynamite.

223
00:13:18,131 --> 00:13:19,475
He's been here all night.

224
00:13:19,499 --> 00:13:21,477
He's just letting
off a little steam.

225
00:13:23,036 --> 00:13:24,346
I've seen enough.

226
00:13:24,370 --> 00:13:25,748
Now please, sir, settle down!

227
00:13:25,772 --> 00:13:27,983
Sorry to drag you out of bed.

228
00:13:28,007 --> 00:13:30,052
Just send us your bill.

229
00:13:30,076 --> 00:13:33,122
It'll be included in my fee.

230
00:13:33,146 --> 00:13:35,057
W-Wait a minute, wait, wait...

231
00:13:35,081 --> 00:13:38,060
You're, you're taking the case?

232
00:13:38,084 --> 00:13:41,597
Well, it's clear as a day
in July he didn't do it.

233
00:13:41,621 --> 00:13:43,599
If Billy had wanted
to kill that man,

234
00:13:43,623 --> 00:13:45,034
he wouldn't have used a gun,

235
00:13:45,058 --> 00:13:48,051
he'd have just
ripped him to pieces.

236
00:14:04,477 --> 00:14:05,654
Well, here you go.

237
00:14:05,678 --> 00:14:06,989
Okay. Okay.

238
00:14:07,013 --> 00:14:11,126
Now, you see, the fight was
postponed till after the trial.

239
00:14:11,150 --> 00:14:13,962
Billy's found guilty,
they'll cancel it altogether.

240
00:14:13,986 --> 00:14:15,998
You like to go to the
fights, don't you, Bob?

241
00:14:16,022 --> 00:14:18,534
Well, I wouldn't mind
if you got Billy off.

242
00:14:18,558 --> 00:14:22,271
Oh... Well, I'll give
it my best shot.

243
00:14:24,630 --> 00:14:27,109
Well...

244
00:14:27,133 --> 00:14:30,145
Looks like Mr. Underwood
was sitting here reading

245
00:14:30,169 --> 00:14:32,648
when he got shot.

246
00:14:32,672 --> 00:14:36,051
Eh... Bullet was
lodged in this chair?

247
00:14:36,075 --> 00:14:38,387
Yeah. Came from
Billy Leon's gun.

248
00:14:38,411 --> 00:14:41,223
We just got the
ballistics report.

249
00:14:41,247 --> 00:14:44,693
This, uh,
Mr. Underwood's briefcase?

250
00:14:44,717 --> 00:14:46,549
Found it in the closet.

251
00:14:48,321 --> 00:14:50,466
That's odd; you'd think
a sportswriter would have

252
00:14:50,490 --> 00:14:52,334
a notepad or a piece
of paper or something

253
00:14:52,358 --> 00:14:53,669
to write on, wouldn't you?

254
00:14:53,693 --> 00:14:56,605
Well, apparently, he didn't
write any of his stories.

255
00:14:56,629 --> 00:15:00,609
He dictated them into this.

256
00:15:00,633 --> 00:15:02,745
Secretary back in New
York transcribed them.

257
00:15:02,769 --> 00:15:03,779
Uh-huh.

258
00:15:03,803 --> 00:15:04,947
Where'd that come from?

259
00:15:04,971 --> 00:15:06,515
- Briefcase.
- Uh-huh.

260
00:15:06,539 --> 00:15:09,485
Now, apparently, it served
as a kind of diary as well.

261
00:15:09,509 --> 00:15:11,353
He was recording what had
happened earlier that night

262
00:15:11,377 --> 00:15:13,055
when somebody called him.

263
00:15:13,079 --> 00:15:14,723
We think it was the killer.

264
00:15:14,747 --> 00:15:15,958
Yeah?

265
00:15:15,982 --> 00:15:18,627
This is what's at
the end of the tape.

266
00:15:18,651 --> 00:15:19,762
- Hello?
- Yeah.

267
00:15:19,786 --> 00:15:22,064
I thought you might call.

268
00:15:22,088 --> 00:15:24,466
Sure, if you want to.

269
00:15:24,490 --> 00:15:25,467
9:00?

270
00:15:25,491 --> 00:15:26,491
9:00 it is.

271
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:30,272
Bingo.

272
00:15:30,296 --> 00:15:32,674
Maid saw Billy Leon running

273
00:15:32,698 --> 00:15:36,378
from the direction of this
bungalow at approximately 9:05.

274
00:15:36,402 --> 00:15:39,566
Oh, daggone.

275
00:15:42,642 --> 00:15:44,153
<i>Bail for the defendant</i>

276
00:15:44,177 --> 00:15:46,422
is set at one million dollars.

277
00:15:46,446 --> 00:15:47,656
Next case.

278
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:50,025
A million dollars?!

279
00:15:50,049 --> 00:15:52,094
With all due
respect, Your Honor,

280
00:15:52,118 --> 00:15:53,629
don't you think that's
a little excessive?

281
00:15:53,653 --> 00:15:55,330
- Ben...
- My client has

282
00:15:55,354 --> 00:15:58,033
no criminal record
and was a national hero

283
00:15:58,057 --> 00:16:00,436
when he represented this
country in the Olympics.

284
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:02,538
- Ben, it's okay.
- And he has never shown himself

285
00:16:02,562 --> 00:16:05,207
to be anything less than
an outstanding citizen.

286
00:16:05,231 --> 00:16:06,708
- It's okay.
- Your client is a man

287
00:16:06,732 --> 00:16:09,178
whose violent temper is
a matter of public record,

288
00:16:09,202 --> 00:16:13,015
Mr. Matlock, and whose fists
are registered lethal weapons.

289
00:16:13,039 --> 00:16:15,150
Bail stands.

290
00:16:15,174 --> 00:16:17,085
- Next case.
- Your Honor,

291
00:16:17,109 --> 00:16:20,456
I strenuously object to the
punitive nature of this amount.

292
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:23,125
It's okay, I can pay it.

293
00:16:23,149 --> 00:16:25,360
What?

294
00:16:25,384 --> 00:16:27,729
The man wants a million
bucks, I'll give him a million bucks.

295
00:16:27,753 --> 00:16:29,998
One phone call and it's
in my checking account.

296
00:16:30,022 --> 00:16:31,923
Got a pen?

297
00:16:33,426 --> 00:16:35,895
Your Honor, bail's fine.

298
00:16:41,067 --> 00:16:45,113
Ah... Want a
little piece of pie?

299
00:16:45,137 --> 00:16:47,549
Oh, no, I better not.

300
00:16:47,573 --> 00:16:49,384
- Little coffee, though?
- Oh, sure.

301
00:16:49,408 --> 00:16:50,686
- Okay...
- Yeah. Thanks.

302
00:16:50,710 --> 00:16:53,188
There you go.

303
00:16:53,212 --> 00:16:56,291
You know, I've seen this
Billy Leon fight a few times.

304
00:16:56,315 --> 00:16:57,693
- Uh-huh.
- He's pretty good.

305
00:16:57,717 --> 00:17:00,295
- Yeah.
- But he's got one weakness.

306
00:17:00,319 --> 00:17:02,397
- Yeah?
- He's a sucker for a left hook.

307
00:17:02,421 --> 00:17:04,233
- Is he?
- Oh, yeah.

308
00:17:04,257 --> 00:17:06,068
You keep throwing that
right, you make yourself

309
00:17:06,092 --> 00:17:08,070
a sucker for a left hook.

310
00:17:08,094 --> 00:17:10,563
I know that from
my own boxing days.

311
00:17:13,566 --> 00:17:15,410
I didn't know you were a boxer.

312
00:17:15,434 --> 00:17:20,749
Oh, yeah, neighborhood club
stuff, but I fought pinweight.

313
00:17:20,773 --> 00:17:23,185
Pinweight?

314
00:17:23,209 --> 00:17:25,087
I thought flyweight
was the lightest.

315
00:17:25,111 --> 00:17:26,288
No, pinweight.

316
00:17:26,312 --> 00:17:28,323
I fought pinweight.

317
00:17:28,347 --> 00:17:30,993
Anyway, let me
show you what I mean.

318
00:17:31,017 --> 00:17:33,529
Come on, put 'em up. Come on.

319
00:17:36,122 --> 00:17:38,333
Okay, now...

320
00:17:38,357 --> 00:17:41,703
in slow motion, throw
a right at my chin.

321
00:17:41,727 --> 00:17:43,059
Go on.

322
00:17:45,665 --> 00:17:52,214
Now I see it coming,
so I tilt my head.

323
00:17:52,238 --> 00:17:56,218
And now your
right jaw's exposed,

324
00:17:56,242 --> 00:17:59,021
so here comes the left hook.

325
00:17:59,045 --> 00:18:00,589
- Pow!
- Yeah.

326
00:18:00,613 --> 00:18:02,024
- See that?
- Yeah.

327
00:18:02,048 --> 00:18:03,926
That's being a
sucker for a left hook.

328
00:18:03,950 --> 00:18:04,927
Yeah, that's good.

329
00:18:04,951 --> 00:18:06,962
Okay now, let's do
it for real this time.

330
00:18:06,986 --> 00:18:08,366
Les, I don't think we
ought to be doing...

331
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:08,964
Come on, come on!

332
00:18:08,988 --> 00:18:10,632
Don't worry, I won't
hurt you, I'll stop short.

333
00:18:10,656 --> 00:18:12,234
- Go on, throw it!
- Okay.

334
00:18:12,258 --> 00:18:15,470
Ooh! Oh... Oh...
I'm so... I'm sorry!

335
00:18:15,494 --> 00:18:17,539
Are, are, are you all
right? Are you all right?

336
00:18:17,563 --> 00:18:19,575
Yeah, I'm fine,
I'm fine, I'm fine.

337
00:18:19,599 --> 00:18:21,343
You walked right
into that, you know.

338
00:18:21,367 --> 00:18:22,711
I know, I know.

339
00:18:22,735 --> 00:18:24,980
You didn't do it right.

340
00:18:25,004 --> 00:18:27,215
- Ben?
- Uh, in here.

341
00:18:27,239 --> 00:18:28,216
I better go.

342
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:29,651
You don't wanna
finish your coffee?

343
00:18:29,675 --> 00:18:31,735
I have coffee at home!

344
00:18:32,745 --> 00:18:35,010
- Oh, hey, hi.
- Hi.

345
00:18:38,050 --> 00:18:40,062
What happened to him?

346
00:18:40,086 --> 00:18:42,631
Just one of life's tragedies.

347
00:18:42,655 --> 00:18:44,466
- What have you got?
- Transcripts.

348
00:18:44,490 --> 00:18:46,134
Just in from New York.

349
00:18:46,158 --> 00:18:48,537
All the tapes Nick Underwood
made in the last month.

350
00:18:48,561 --> 00:18:50,472
The man could sure talk.

351
00:18:50,496 --> 00:18:52,341
What do you suppose
we got in there?

352
00:18:52,365 --> 00:18:53,675
Secretary said he'd been working

353
00:18:53,699 --> 00:18:55,544
the last three weeks,
day and night, to try to

354
00:18:55,568 --> 00:18:57,679
figure out who'd fed him
that fake steroid story.

355
00:18:57,703 --> 00:18:59,348
- Have any luck?
- Plenty.

356
00:18:59,372 --> 00:19:01,083
Apparently, his next
article was going to reveal

357
00:19:01,107 --> 00:19:03,919
what he found out about some
of the people closest to Billy.

358
00:19:03,943 --> 00:19:04,920
Who?

359
00:19:04,944 --> 00:19:08,056
His promoter Clayton Ross,
his trainer Mickey Callahan,

360
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:09,958
and his wife Alana.

361
00:19:09,982 --> 00:19:12,260
Secretary said he
had dirt on them all.

362
00:19:12,284 --> 00:19:14,529
This is the article right here.

363
00:19:14,553 --> 00:19:18,166
Oh... well, as Sherlock
Holmes used to say,

364
00:19:18,190 --> 00:19:20,969
we've got ourselves
three suspects.

365
00:19:20,993 --> 00:19:23,338
Did Sherlock Holmes
really say that?

366
00:19:23,362 --> 00:19:25,073
I don't know... but
it would've been

367
00:19:25,097 --> 00:19:27,275
a good line for
him, wouldn't it?

368
00:19:31,537 --> 00:19:33,482
Come in, Mr. Matlock.
Please, sit.

369
00:19:33,506 --> 00:19:35,283
- Right...?
- Yes, fine, thank you.

370
00:19:35,307 --> 00:19:37,152
Were we in my real
office, up in Chicago,

371
00:19:37,176 --> 00:19:38,954
I could offer you any
number of beverages,

372
00:19:38,978 --> 00:19:40,522
but since I'm just
renting this place

373
00:19:40,546 --> 00:19:42,538
while we're in Atlanta...

374
00:19:43,983 --> 00:19:46,111
Warm diet pop?

375
00:19:47,787 --> 00:19:48,764
No, thanks.

376
00:19:48,788 --> 00:19:51,566
Uh-huh, okay, good.

377
00:19:51,590 --> 00:19:53,081
So...

378
00:19:54,427 --> 00:19:56,204
What can I do you for?

379
00:19:56,228 --> 00:19:57,973
Well, I understand
that Nick Underwood

380
00:19:57,997 --> 00:19:59,741
knew a few things about you

381
00:19:59,765 --> 00:20:04,312
that were, well...
rather embarrassing.

382
00:20:04,336 --> 00:20:07,049
Really? Like what?

383
00:20:07,073 --> 00:20:08,717
He knew about
those fights you fixed

384
00:20:08,741 --> 00:20:11,053
back when Billy was
just getting started.

385
00:20:11,077 --> 00:20:14,489
I don't know what
you're talking about.

386
00:20:14,513 --> 00:20:16,525
You figured all Billy
needed back then

387
00:20:16,549 --> 00:20:18,393
was a good dose
of self-confidence,

388
00:20:18,417 --> 00:20:21,596
so you paid three fighters
to take dives, and it worked.

389
00:20:21,620 --> 00:20:23,384
That's crazy.

390
00:20:25,024 --> 00:20:27,502
Well, it makes
perfect sense to me.

391
00:20:27,526 --> 00:20:30,172
You call Mr. Underwood...
Anonymously, of course...

392
00:20:30,196 --> 00:20:33,008
And give him the names
of people who say they saw

393
00:20:33,032 --> 00:20:36,712
Billy using steroids,
and you pay them to lie

394
00:20:36,736 --> 00:20:39,247
and then sit back and
wait for them to go to press.

395
00:20:39,271 --> 00:20:42,084
But Billy is my
bread and butter.

396
00:20:42,108 --> 00:20:44,119
Why would I go
ruining his career

397
00:20:44,143 --> 00:20:45,587
by planting a story like that?

398
00:20:45,611 --> 00:20:48,156
Because you knew it wasn't true.

399
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:50,459
Once he was tested,
he'd be exonerated.

400
00:20:50,483 --> 00:20:53,061
And all the press would
be millions of dollars

401
00:20:53,085 --> 00:20:54,796
in free publicity.

402
00:20:57,189 --> 00:20:59,768
I would never do
anything like that.

403
00:20:59,792 --> 00:21:02,227
So you're denying
you had an affair?

404
00:21:05,498 --> 00:21:07,242
Once.

405
00:21:07,266 --> 00:21:10,378
I cheated just once.

406
00:21:10,402 --> 00:21:13,582
It was when Billy
was in Tokyo last year

407
00:21:13,606 --> 00:21:15,751
for that exhibition match.

408
00:21:15,775 --> 00:21:20,736
It was short and... not
even particularly sweet.

409
00:21:21,881 --> 00:21:25,327
I don't know how the hell Nick
Underwood found out about that.

410
00:21:25,351 --> 00:21:26,695
Well, he did.

411
00:21:26,719 --> 00:21:28,597
And if he'd gone public
with what he knew,

412
00:21:28,621 --> 00:21:30,632
Billy would've divorced you
and left you without a penny,

413
00:21:30,656 --> 00:21:32,067
wouldn't he?

414
00:21:32,091 --> 00:21:34,402
He would've tossed
me out on the spot.

415
00:21:34,426 --> 00:21:36,204
He hates getting lied to.

416
00:21:36,228 --> 00:21:40,041
And fixing a fight is about
as big a lie as you can get.

417
00:21:40,065 --> 00:21:43,645
So you'd have lost a job
that pays what, six figures?

418
00:21:43,669 --> 00:21:46,248
I can't give you an
exact figure, but, uh...

419
00:21:46,272 --> 00:21:48,517
But if Billy'd
cancelled that fight

420
00:21:48,541 --> 00:21:50,485
to get back at you
for planting that story,

421
00:21:50,509 --> 00:21:52,587
you'd have been out a
lot of money, wouldn't you?

422
00:21:52,611 --> 00:21:54,489
Yeah, sure, right.

423
00:21:54,513 --> 00:21:57,125
But if you're suggesting
I killed Nick Underwood

424
00:21:57,149 --> 00:21:59,027
in order to keep that from
happening, you're wrong.

425
00:21:59,051 --> 00:22:02,464
I was in the hotel ballroom
that night, hosting a reception.

426
00:22:02,488 --> 00:22:03,899
You never left?

427
00:22:03,923 --> 00:22:05,734
Don't you think
somebody would've noticed

428
00:22:05,758 --> 00:22:07,369
if I left my own party?

429
00:22:07,393 --> 00:22:09,671
Not if you were only
gone a few minutes.

430
00:22:09,695 --> 00:22:12,440
Well, I arrived
just after it began,

431
00:22:12,464 --> 00:22:15,377
and I didn't leave until
the sirens started going by.

432
00:22:15,401 --> 00:22:17,145
Then I went outside
with everyone else

433
00:22:17,169 --> 00:22:19,080
to see what was going on.

434
00:22:19,104 --> 00:22:22,050
Did you hang out with anyone in
particular while you were there?

435
00:22:22,074 --> 00:22:23,718
Usually when I
go to these things,

436
00:22:23,742 --> 00:22:25,053
all I do is eat and drink.

437
00:22:25,077 --> 00:22:26,221
Mostly eat.

438
00:22:26,245 --> 00:22:27,923
I don't socialize too good.

439
00:22:27,947 --> 00:22:31,526
So no one can say for sure that
you were there the whole time?

440
00:22:31,550 --> 00:22:34,315
Yeah, someone
can say that... me.

441
00:22:36,889 --> 00:22:39,768
Look, I got a lot of
work to do, okay?

442
00:22:39,792 --> 00:22:42,671
Oh, I understand
you're a very busy man.

443
00:22:42,695 --> 00:22:44,439
You know, promoting fights

444
00:22:44,463 --> 00:22:46,641
is what a lot of people
claim we lawyers do.

445
00:22:47,666 --> 00:22:49,477
Well, you just give me a holler

446
00:22:49,501 --> 00:22:51,346
if there's anything
else I could do for you.

447
00:22:51,370 --> 00:22:52,714
Okay.

448
00:22:56,208 --> 00:22:59,007
- Bye, Mr. Ross.
- Bye-bye.

449
00:23:01,814 --> 00:23:04,443
Good-bye, Alana.

450
00:23:17,129 --> 00:23:19,428
- I'll see you later, Mickey.
- Yeah.

451
00:23:30,476 --> 00:23:32,775
Anybody here yet?

452
00:23:45,524 --> 00:23:47,516
Anybody here?

453
00:23:56,702 --> 00:23:57,795
Anybody here?

454
00:23:58,837 --> 00:24:00,348
I'm here!

455
00:24:00,372 --> 00:24:01,806
I'm here.

456
00:24:02,841 --> 00:24:03,985
I got great news.

457
00:24:04,009 --> 00:24:05,387
- So do I.
- Me, too.

458
00:24:05,411 --> 00:24:07,880
I think I know
who the killer is!

459
00:24:13,585 --> 00:24:14,863
<i>They all had motive.</i>

460
00:24:14,887 --> 00:24:16,197
- Yes, they all had motive.
- They all had access

461
00:24:16,221 --> 00:24:17,565
- to Billy's gun.
- They all could've left the party at...

462
00:24:17,589 --> 00:24:19,200
- They all could've left the party...
- They all had motive.

463
00:24:19,224 --> 00:24:20,702
They all could've left
the party and committed

464
00:24:20,726 --> 00:24:22,137
- the murder, yes.
- And they all had access

465
00:24:22,161 --> 00:24:23,872
- to Billy's gun, I know.
- And all the arguing

466
00:24:23,896 --> 00:24:25,540
and the background
material in the world

467
00:24:25,564 --> 00:24:27,142
isn't gonna tell us
which one of them did it.

468
00:24:27,166 --> 00:24:29,544
- No...
- Ben?!

469
00:24:29,568 --> 00:24:31,112
Uh, in here, Les.

470
00:24:31,136 --> 00:24:34,316
Oh! Your door was open,
so I thought you were...

471
00:24:34,340 --> 00:24:36,985
Oh, you're busy.

472
00:24:37,009 --> 00:24:38,386
Well, yeah, kind of.

473
00:24:38,410 --> 00:24:40,488
We're having a little
disagreement over who the

474
00:24:40,512 --> 00:24:42,557
real killer is in the
Billy Leon case.

475
00:24:42,581 --> 00:24:44,726
- Ah, the old deadlock, huh?
- Yeah.

476
00:24:44,750 --> 00:24:46,761
- What's up?
- Well, you know the

477
00:24:46,785 --> 00:24:50,565
other night when I was giving
you those boxing pointers?

478
00:24:50,589 --> 00:24:52,367
Oh, I'm sorry about that.

479
00:24:52,391 --> 00:24:53,902
No, no, no, it's all right.

480
00:24:53,926 --> 00:24:58,039
Boxing is one thing, but
defending yourself is another.

481
00:24:58,063 --> 00:25:01,042
- So, you know what I use?
- What?

482
00:25:01,066 --> 00:25:02,577
Karate.

483
00:25:02,601 --> 00:25:04,579
You know karate?

484
00:25:04,603 --> 00:25:06,748
Are you kidding?
I'm a gray belt.

485
00:25:06,772 --> 00:25:08,049
What's a gray belt?

486
00:25:08,073 --> 00:25:09,384
Senior citizens.

487
00:25:09,408 --> 00:25:12,087
Now, Benj, I want you
to pick up that ruler,

488
00:25:12,111 --> 00:25:15,223
pretend it's a knife.

489
00:25:15,247 --> 00:25:16,324
This ruler's a knife?

490
00:25:16,348 --> 00:25:17,559
Yeah, pretend, pretend, pretend.

491
00:25:17,583 --> 00:25:18,760
Okay.

492
00:25:18,784 --> 00:25:20,395
Now, in slow motion,

493
00:25:20,419 --> 00:25:23,531
I want you come at
me and try to stab me.

494
00:25:23,555 --> 00:25:24,866
Oh, Les, I don't think we...

495
00:25:24,890 --> 00:25:26,167
Oh, come on, Ben.

496
00:25:26,191 --> 00:25:28,470
Slow motion.

497
00:25:28,494 --> 00:25:29,838
Hah!

498
00:25:29,862 --> 00:25:31,773
Hah?

499
00:25:31,797 --> 00:25:33,475
Karate. Hah!

500
00:25:33,499 --> 00:25:36,128
Okay. Here I come.

501
00:25:42,775 --> 00:25:44,953
See there?

502
00:25:44,977 --> 00:25:46,988
- That's a karate block.
- Huh.

503
00:25:47,012 --> 00:25:48,289
- See?
- Yeah.

504
00:25:48,313 --> 00:25:49,591
You like that?

505
00:25:49,615 --> 00:25:52,293
Now I just take my other hand

506
00:25:52,317 --> 00:25:54,596
and I form a vise.

507
00:25:54,620 --> 00:25:57,065
And now your hand is

508
00:25:57,089 --> 00:25:59,768
completely tied up, right?

509
00:25:59,792 --> 00:26:01,870
- Pretty good.
- Yeah.

510
00:26:01,894 --> 00:26:04,706
All right, now, forget
the slow motion

511
00:26:04,730 --> 00:26:06,841
- and really try to stab me.
- Well, Les...

512
00:26:06,865 --> 00:26:09,177
Come on! No, no, it's karate.

513
00:26:09,201 --> 00:26:10,612
Come on, give it
everything you got.

514
00:26:10,636 --> 00:26:11,613
Hah!

515
00:26:11,637 --> 00:26:13,265
Okay.

516
00:26:13,972 --> 00:26:17,018
Hah! Hah!

517
00:26:17,042 --> 00:26:18,253
Hah!

518
00:26:18,277 --> 00:26:20,021
Hah... Aah...!

519
00:26:27,986 --> 00:26:30,465
You-You want to let me up, Ben?

520
00:26:30,489 --> 00:26:32,567
What?

521
00:26:32,591 --> 00:26:34,069
Let me up?

522
00:26:34,093 --> 00:26:36,255
Oh, okay.

523
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:43,578
Are you all right?

524
00:26:43,602 --> 00:26:45,346
Yeah, fine, fine.

525
00:26:45,370 --> 00:26:47,115
You didn't do it right.

526
00:26:47,139 --> 00:26:50,118
- I'm sorry.
- It's all right.

527
00:26:50,142 --> 00:26:54,489
Uh... well, uh, you
people go back to work.

528
00:26:54,513 --> 00:26:57,108
And, uh, as you were.

529
00:27:03,922 --> 00:27:06,968
Why does he always
do things like that?

530
00:27:06,992 --> 00:27:08,790
One of life's tragedies.

531
00:27:15,334 --> 00:27:16,411
Wait a minute.

532
00:27:16,435 --> 00:27:18,768
He's wearing reading glasses.

533
00:27:23,775 --> 00:27:26,688
A lot of people wear
reading glasses.

534
00:27:26,712 --> 00:27:31,025
Yeah, but when the
police found his body,

535
00:27:31,049 --> 00:27:33,962
his glasses were
in his briefcase

536
00:27:33,986 --> 00:27:35,864
and his briefcase
was in the closet.

537
00:27:35,888 --> 00:27:38,066
Well, maybe he was
wearing contacts.

538
00:27:38,090 --> 00:27:40,702
Not according to the coroner.

539
00:27:40,726 --> 00:27:42,695
Hmm.

540
00:27:44,897 --> 00:27:48,810
Maybe he wasn't just
sitting there reading the paper

541
00:27:48,834 --> 00:27:51,179
when the killer walked in.

542
00:27:51,203 --> 00:27:54,215
Maybe the killer
wanted it to look like

543
00:27:54,239 --> 00:27:57,334
he was sitting there
reading the paper.

544
00:27:59,545 --> 00:28:02,290
We know from the bullet hole

545
00:28:02,314 --> 00:28:05,026
that Mr. Underwood
was sitting in that chair

546
00:28:05,050 --> 00:28:06,694
when he was shot.

547
00:28:06,718 --> 00:28:09,731
What we don't know
is where the chair was.

548
00:28:09,755 --> 00:28:12,000
It may not have
been at the desk?

549
00:28:12,024 --> 00:28:15,170
That chair's on casters.

550
00:28:15,194 --> 00:28:19,325
We don't even know
if it was in his room.

551
00:28:25,337 --> 00:28:26,314
<i>Impossible.</i>

552
00:28:26,338 --> 00:28:28,950
But there-there must
be a way of telling

553
00:28:28,974 --> 00:28:32,053
if this chair was moved in
here from another bungalow.

554
00:28:32,077 --> 00:28:33,655
Well, not really, you see.

555
00:28:33,679 --> 00:28:35,657
The chairs were ordered
at exactly the same time,

556
00:28:35,681 --> 00:28:37,625
in exactly the same style

557
00:28:37,649 --> 00:28:40,828
and upholstered in
exactly the same fabric.

558
00:28:40,852 --> 00:28:43,031
They are, to all
intents and purposes,

559
00:28:43,055 --> 00:28:45,733
absolutely identical.

560
00:28:45,757 --> 00:28:47,635
Are they marked to tell
what bungalow they're from?

561
00:28:47,659 --> 00:28:49,837
Uh, there would be no need.

562
00:28:49,861 --> 00:28:51,239
Well, would any of
your housekeeping staff

563
00:28:51,263 --> 00:28:52,507
be able to tell them apart?

564
00:28:52,531 --> 00:28:53,708
I don't think so.

565
00:28:53,732 --> 00:28:56,844
Maybe they'd recognize a
rip or a tear or something.

566
00:28:56,868 --> 00:28:58,413
Well, of course, you
are most welcome to ask,

567
00:28:58,437 --> 00:29:01,449
but I do think
it's most unlikely.

568
00:29:01,473 --> 00:29:03,874
I'm really very sorry.

569
00:29:05,177 --> 00:29:08,204
I really wish I could
be a little more positive.

570
00:29:10,015 --> 00:29:12,493
Well, we got three of them.

571
00:29:12,517 --> 00:29:15,363
Same motive, same opportunity

572
00:29:15,387 --> 00:29:17,832
and the very same
chair in every room.

573
00:29:17,856 --> 00:29:20,101
- Mr. Matlock.
- Huh?

574
00:29:20,125 --> 00:29:21,236
Mr. Matlock.

575
00:29:21,260 --> 00:29:23,271
I, uh... hello. I don't
believe we've met.

576
00:29:23,295 --> 00:29:24,706
- I'm Clayton Ross.
- That's Conrad McMasters.

577
00:29:24,730 --> 00:29:25,974
Michelle Thomas.

578
00:29:25,998 --> 00:29:27,875
Great. Pleasure. I...
I was looking for you

579
00:29:27,899 --> 00:29:29,944
when they told me in the
office that I might find you

580
00:29:29,968 --> 00:29:31,145
- out here.
- Well, yeah.

581
00:29:31,169 --> 00:29:33,214
Well, I have
something to say to you.

582
00:29:33,238 --> 00:29:34,816
Something rather urgent.

583
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:36,684
- May-May I speak freely?
- Oh, of course.

584
00:29:36,708 --> 00:29:38,820
- Of course.
- Our conversation the other day

585
00:29:38,844 --> 00:29:40,922
got me to thinking
about the party I threw

586
00:29:40,946 --> 00:29:42,657
the night of the
murder, and suddenly,

587
00:29:42,681 --> 00:29:44,058
I remembered something.

588
00:29:44,082 --> 00:29:45,193
What's that?

589
00:29:45,217 --> 00:29:48,930
I remember seeing
a red stain on the...

590
00:29:48,954 --> 00:29:51,466
on the right sleeve of
Mickey Callahan's suit.

591
00:29:51,490 --> 00:29:53,234
Ri-Ri-Right... right about here.

592
00:29:53,258 --> 00:29:54,402
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

593
00:29:54,426 --> 00:29:56,938
Any idea, uh, what
time you saw that?

594
00:29:56,962 --> 00:29:58,473
Yeah. Well, it was...

595
00:29:58,497 --> 00:30:00,174
it was just before
the police arrived,

596
00:30:00,198 --> 00:30:02,010
because I remember
I was just about

597
00:30:02,034 --> 00:30:03,511
to say something
to him about it,

598
00:30:03,535 --> 00:30:06,247
but then being interrupted
by the, uh, sirens.

599
00:30:06,271 --> 00:30:09,050
So, it was about, uh,
ten after 9:00, then?

600
00:30:09,074 --> 00:30:10,351
Sure.

601
00:30:10,375 --> 00:30:11,919
I realize this
could be construed

602
00:30:11,943 --> 00:30:13,788
as a rather transparent
attempt on my part

603
00:30:13,812 --> 00:30:15,990
to cast suspicion on
another human being, but I...

604
00:30:16,014 --> 00:30:17,258
Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

605
00:30:17,282 --> 00:30:18,993
We know you're just
trying to be helpful,

606
00:30:19,017 --> 00:30:20,828
- and we appreciate it.
- Good.

607
00:30:20,852 --> 00:30:21,829
- Yeah.
- Good.

608
00:30:21,853 --> 00:30:23,898
Good. Well, if I
could, uh, be helpful

609
00:30:23,922 --> 00:30:25,366
- in any other way, um...
- Yeah.

610
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:26,534
- Let-let me know.
- Okay.

611
00:30:26,558 --> 00:30:28,102
Nice to have met you.

612
00:30:33,732 --> 00:30:36,511
I want you to watch
him like a hawk.

613
00:30:36,535 --> 00:30:38,012
I'll be all over him.

614
00:30:38,036 --> 00:30:39,732
Yeah.

615
00:30:42,407 --> 00:30:45,286
He could be telling the truth.

616
00:30:45,310 --> 00:30:47,302
Yeah.

617
00:30:49,514 --> 00:30:50,692
Stick him, Nate. Stick.

618
00:30:50,716 --> 00:30:51,759
Don't block with your head.

619
00:30:51,783 --> 00:30:52,827
Tuck your head.

620
00:30:52,851 --> 00:30:54,062
All right, get off of there.

621
00:30:54,086 --> 00:30:55,396
Get off of there! Pretty!

622
00:30:55,420 --> 00:30:57,165
Who's the champ, Billy? Come on.

623
00:30:57,189 --> 00:30:58,933
That's it. That's it.

624
00:30:58,957 --> 00:31:00,735
Work, work, work, work.

625
00:31:00,759 --> 00:31:02,036
- Don't stand there!
- I don't know.

626
00:31:02,060 --> 00:31:03,905
He's taking too many
shots in the face, Mick.

627
00:31:03,929 --> 00:31:05,139
Come on, tell me about it.

628
00:31:05,163 --> 00:31:06,426
His mind ain't here today.

629
00:31:07,466 --> 00:31:08,743
Hey, Ben!

630
00:31:08,767 --> 00:31:10,167
What say, Billy?

631
00:31:11,203 --> 00:31:12,680
All right... break.

632
00:31:12,704 --> 00:31:15,416
I-I'm Ben Matlock,
uh, Billy's lawyer.

633
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:17,085
- Hi.
- You must be Mickey Callahan.

634
00:31:17,109 --> 00:31:20,688
Yeah, I know who I
am, but who is this?

635
00:31:20,712 --> 00:31:22,423
Michelle Thomas.

636
00:31:22,447 --> 00:31:24,258
Hi.

637
00:31:24,282 --> 00:31:26,327
Don't be embarrassed.

638
00:31:26,351 --> 00:31:28,696
I react like that every day.

639
00:31:28,720 --> 00:31:30,898
Could I speak with
you for a moment?

640
00:31:30,922 --> 00:31:32,433
- Yeah, sure.
- Alone?

641
00:31:32,457 --> 00:31:34,235
Yeah. Uh, Freddy!

642
00:31:34,259 --> 00:31:35,370
Do me a favor, huh?

643
00:31:35,394 --> 00:31:37,105
Get Billy on the
heavy bag for a bit.

644
00:31:37,129 --> 00:31:38,339
Work him. I'll be right back.

645
00:31:38,363 --> 00:31:39,907
Okay. All right,
Billy, let's go.

646
00:31:39,931 --> 00:31:40,931
Come on.

647
00:31:44,035 --> 00:31:46,061
Coming along real nice, champ.

648
00:31:48,340 --> 00:31:49,784
You mind if I sit down?

649
00:31:49,808 --> 00:31:51,185
Go ahead.

650
00:31:51,209 --> 00:31:53,421
Come on, champ! Work, work!

651
00:31:53,445 --> 00:31:55,223
You always watch him work out?

652
00:31:55,247 --> 00:31:56,290
Always.

653
00:31:56,314 --> 00:31:57,625
That's it! Forward.

654
00:31:57,649 --> 00:31:59,193
Work, work!

655
00:31:59,217 --> 00:32:01,162
The perfect wife.

656
00:32:01,186 --> 00:32:03,164
No, Ms. Thomas.

657
00:32:03,188 --> 00:32:06,501
I'm just trying to make
up for not being perfect.

658
00:32:06,525 --> 00:32:08,336
Work! Work!

659
00:32:08,360 --> 00:32:09,919
Work!

660
00:32:10,962 --> 00:32:12,173
Well, I'll be darned.

661
00:32:12,197 --> 00:32:14,375
I did get something
on this sleeve here.

662
00:32:14,399 --> 00:32:16,644
Oh, yeah. Ah.

663
00:32:16,668 --> 00:32:17,912
Any idea what it is?

664
00:32:17,936 --> 00:32:19,113
Well, I was drinking beer.

665
00:32:19,137 --> 00:32:20,348
It can't be wine or anything.

666
00:32:20,372 --> 00:32:22,250
Yeah?

667
00:32:23,708 --> 00:32:25,319
What were you eating?

668
00:32:25,343 --> 00:32:26,554
Whatever they were serving.

669
00:32:26,578 --> 00:32:27,922
I'm not what you call picky.

670
00:32:27,946 --> 00:32:29,257
Uh-huh.

671
00:32:29,281 --> 00:32:30,691
They serve shrimp?

672
00:32:30,715 --> 00:32:32,326
- Sure.
- You like shrimp?

673
00:32:32,350 --> 00:32:33,628
Oh yeah.

674
00:32:33,652 --> 00:32:36,030
- Cocktail sauce?
- All the time.

675
00:32:36,054 --> 00:32:38,566
'Cause that's
what I think this is.

676
00:32:39,958 --> 00:32:42,870
See, that stain could've
proved you were the killer.

677
00:32:42,894 --> 00:32:45,706
Instead, it just proves
you're kind of messy.

678
00:32:47,432 --> 00:32:49,177
Ain't that the truth, huh?

679
00:32:49,201 --> 00:32:50,645
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

680
00:32:50,669 --> 00:32:54,282
Yeah... Yeah...

681
00:32:54,306 --> 00:32:57,418
Anything, uh...
anything about that night

682
00:32:57,442 --> 00:33:01,155
you, uh, happen to remember?

683
00:33:01,179 --> 00:33:04,358
No. I... I can't say
that there is. I...

684
00:33:04,382 --> 00:33:06,160
Well, did, uh...
did Clayton Ross

685
00:33:06,184 --> 00:33:09,330
and Mrs. Leon stay at the
party till the police arrived?

686
00:33:09,354 --> 00:33:10,731
Well, as far as I know.

687
00:33:10,755 --> 00:33:12,133
Alana and I went together.

688
00:33:12,157 --> 00:33:14,135
Their bungalow's
right next door.

689
00:33:14,159 --> 00:33:16,437
Uh, but once we got inside,
we split up pretty quick.

690
00:33:16,461 --> 00:33:19,040
I sort of made a
beeline for the food.

691
00:33:19,064 --> 00:33:20,341
Yeah.

692
00:33:20,365 --> 00:33:22,410
Yeah, well, I'll let you
get back to the gym.

693
00:33:22,434 --> 00:33:24,745
Billy's not going to
have much time to train

694
00:33:24,769 --> 00:33:26,047
once the trial starts.

695
00:33:26,071 --> 00:33:27,648
Oh, he doesn't need
any more training.

696
00:33:27,672 --> 00:33:28,950
I mean, the kid is there.

697
00:33:28,974 --> 00:33:29,918
He's ready.

698
00:33:29,942 --> 00:33:31,452
It looked that way to me.

699
00:33:31,476 --> 00:33:32,753
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

700
00:33:32,777 --> 00:33:34,689
Okay.

701
00:33:34,713 --> 00:33:38,059
Uh, Mr... Mr. Matlock,
uh... uh, listen...

702
00:33:38,083 --> 00:33:43,331
I... I know that you know
about how I fixed those fights.

703
00:33:43,355 --> 00:33:46,868
I just want to thank
you for not telling Billy.

704
00:33:46,892 --> 00:33:50,304
Well, the trial starts tomorrow.

705
00:33:50,328 --> 00:33:53,765
I wouldn't be thanking me yet.

706
00:34:32,437 --> 00:34:35,416
Ben, are you all right?

707
00:34:35,440 --> 00:34:37,718
What are you staring at?

708
00:34:37,742 --> 00:34:40,906
Something I should
have seen a long time ago.

709
00:34:48,453 --> 00:34:50,431
<i>These chairs are all alike.</i>

710
00:34:50,455 --> 00:34:52,967
Nothing is exactly alike.

711
00:34:52,991 --> 00:34:55,269
- Whew! Boy.
- It's not possible.

712
00:34:55,293 --> 00:34:57,705
Boy, it is so hot in here.

713
00:34:57,729 --> 00:35:00,563
Yeah. Yeah.

714
00:35:03,234 --> 00:35:05,032
Yeah.

715
00:35:08,640 --> 00:35:12,008
This window faces south.

716
00:35:19,250 --> 00:35:23,731
And the sun has been shining...

717
00:35:23,755 --> 00:35:27,702
in here all afternoon.

718
00:35:27,726 --> 00:35:30,004
Yep. Yep.

719
00:35:30,028 --> 00:35:33,107
Yep, yep, yep.

720
00:35:33,131 --> 00:35:35,191
- Let's go.
- Hmm.

721
00:35:36,501 --> 00:35:39,113
You may call your first
witness, Mr. Matlock.

722
00:35:39,137 --> 00:35:43,507
Defense calls Alana
Leon to the stand.

723
00:35:54,519 --> 00:35:57,956
I thought you said her testimony
wouldn't do us any good.

724
00:35:59,324 --> 00:36:01,316
I was wrong.

725
00:36:02,460 --> 00:36:03,871
<i>How long</i>

726
00:36:03,895 --> 00:36:06,474
have you and Billy
been married, Mrs. Leon?

727
00:36:06,498 --> 00:36:07,842
Three years.

728
00:36:07,866 --> 00:36:10,011
How'd you meet?

729
00:36:10,035 --> 00:36:12,647
I was the hostess at a
restaurant in Phoenix,

730
00:36:12,671 --> 00:36:14,582
and he walked in.

731
00:36:14,606 --> 00:36:16,584
And it was love at first sight.

732
00:36:16,608 --> 00:36:19,510
Ours is a very happy marriage.

733
00:36:24,249 --> 00:36:27,328
Um, I wonder why Nick Underwood

734
00:36:27,352 --> 00:36:29,196
came to the Crown
Hotel so early.

735
00:36:29,220 --> 00:36:32,299
I mean, the fight was
a whole week away.

736
00:36:32,323 --> 00:36:33,401
What was he doing there?

737
00:36:33,425 --> 00:36:35,936
Well, how should I know?

738
00:36:35,960 --> 00:36:39,073
He didn't come to cover
the fight at all, did he?

739
00:36:39,097 --> 00:36:41,642
He came to tell
Billy something that

740
00:36:41,666 --> 00:36:44,145
he had found out about you

741
00:36:44,169 --> 00:36:48,649
and one of your husband's
former sparring partners, Tom Kirby.

742
00:36:48,673 --> 00:36:49,650
Objection.

743
00:36:49,674 --> 00:36:50,885
Calls for speculation.

744
00:36:50,909 --> 00:36:53,087
Sustained.

745
00:36:53,111 --> 00:36:57,674
You had an affair with Tom
Kirby a year ago, didn't you?

746
00:37:01,252 --> 00:37:02,311
Uh...

747
00:37:04,322 --> 00:37:07,601
It was the stupidest
thing I have ever done

748
00:37:07,625 --> 00:37:09,719
in my entire life.

749
00:37:11,396 --> 00:37:13,888
I'm sorry, Billy.

750
00:37:15,300 --> 00:37:17,792
He didn't mean a
thing to me, I swear.

751
00:37:21,139 --> 00:37:23,784
According to
Mr. Underwood's notes,

752
00:37:23,808 --> 00:37:26,787
which his secretaries
transcribed,

753
00:37:26,811 --> 00:37:30,791
he not only knew about
your affair, but was planning

754
00:37:30,815 --> 00:37:33,494
to reveal it in an
upcoming issue

755
00:37:33,518 --> 00:37:34,495
of <i>Sports Week.</i>

756
00:37:34,519 --> 00:37:36,130
And if that had happened,

757
00:37:36,154 --> 00:37:38,699
Billy would've divorced you in
a matter of days, wouldn't he?

758
00:37:38,723 --> 00:37:42,570
Well, I'd like to think that
he would've forgiven me.

759
00:37:42,594 --> 00:37:46,140
Given his temper, he
would've divorced you

760
00:37:46,164 --> 00:37:48,109
in a heartbeat, and you know it.

761
00:37:48,133 --> 00:37:50,711
No more sports cars,
no more diamonds,

762
00:37:50,735 --> 00:37:53,080
no more trips to Paris.

763
00:37:53,104 --> 00:37:56,751
You couldn't handle that.

764
00:37:56,775 --> 00:38:00,287
So you left the
cocktail party that night,

765
00:38:00,311 --> 00:38:01,856
called Nick Underwood,

766
00:38:01,880 --> 00:38:04,024
told him to come
to your bungalow.

767
00:38:04,048 --> 00:38:05,493
